assign_material.set_volume","type":"bool","value":"true"}],"compounds":[{"path":"root","name":"Graphs::Texture::texture_scatter","metadata":{"compoundIsGraph":"true","author":"Autodesk","category":"Texture","description":"This graph illustrates the use of read_texture to drive a scatter density and output color.","documentation":"texture_scatter_docs_${language}.md","_notes":[{"type":"sticky_note","text":"This example graph uses an image that is included with the Bifrost installation. \n\nDisconnect ***expand_env_vars*** then change the *file_path* on the ***read_texture*** compound to a path to the desired image on disk.  "},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"# ***assign_diagnostic_material***\n\nThe texture can be plugged directly into the *emission_color* port of the ***assign_diagnostic_material***.\n\nThe  ***scatter_points*** compound transfers the UVs from the input mesh which means that many of Bifrost's auto-ports can now sample using those UVs.\n\n"},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"# ***scatter_points***\n\nThe image coming from the ***read_texture*** compound is being used as the density weights for the scatter.  This means the texture output is controlling the probability of a point being scattered at that position.  This samples the input image at a much-higher resolution than the image itself.\n\n"}],"_backdrops":[{"type":"backdrop","title":"Path to image"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Do scatter"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Render"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Display"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Display and render"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Output"}]},"ports":[],"terminal_states":[{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::final","enabled":"true"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::diagnostic","enabled":"true"}],"node_count":9,"connection_count":10,"value_count":50}],"teaching_notes":[{"type":"sticky_note","text":"This example graph uses an image that is included with the Bifrost installation. \n\nDisconnect ***expand_env_vars*** then change the *file_path* on the ***read_texture*** compound to a path to the desired image on disk.  "},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"# ***assign_diagnostic_material***\n\nThe texture can be plugged directly into the *emission_color* port of the ***assign_diagnostic_material***.\n\nThe  ***scatter_points*** compound transfers the UVs from the input mesh which means that many of Bifrost's auto-ports can now sample using those UVs.\n\n"},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"# ***scatter_points***\n\nThe image coming from the ***read_texture*** compound is being used as the density weights for the scatter.  This means the texture output is controlling the probability of a point being scattered at that position.  This samples the input image at a much-higher resolution than the image itself.\n\n"}]}